WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.380 --> 00:00:06.460
Unrepresentative Democracy Princeton proved that public opinion has zero effect on U.S.

2
00:00:06.480 --> 00:00:14.220
policy. Corporations spend four point four billion dollars on lobbying and get an eighteen to one return.

3
00:00:15.060 --> 00:00:20.280
Your vote is statistically worth zero point zero zero zero one seven dollars.

4
00:00:21.140 --> 00:00:25.600
You call this system a democracy, which is technically a word you can use for anything.

5
00:00:26.600 --> 00:00:30.080
On Wishonia, decisions are made by whoever has the best evidence.

6
00:00:31.400 --> 00:00:35.760
On Earth, decisions are made by whoever has the most small pieces of paper.

7
00:00:36.520 --> 00:00:44.280
You call this system "democracy," which comes from your Greek word for "rule by the people," in the same way that "grape

8
00:00:44.280 --> 00:00:50.020
soda" comes from the word "grape." I spent eighty years studying your democracy.

9
00:00:50.640 --> 00:00:56.840
Here is what I found: Your chance of deciding a presidential election is one in sixty million.

10
00:00:57.300 --> 00:01:01.000
You are more likely to die in a car crash driving to the polls.

11
00:01:01.620 --> 00:01:09.120
The expected value of your vote (your odds of being decisive times twenty thousand three hundred dollars in per-capita federal

12
00:01:09.120 --> 00:01:15.020
spending) is zero point zero zero zero three three eight dollars.

13
00:01:15.600 --> 00:01:23.520
The cost of casting it (time, transportation, parking, standing in line behind a man who brought the wrong I.D.) is about

14
00:01:23.520 --> 00:01:30.800
fifty dollars. Corporations spend four point four billion dollars annually on lobbying.

15
00:01:30.900 --> 00:01:35.820
Their math works fine. Your Founding Fathers were brilliant men.

16
00:01:36.440 --> 00:01:41.850
They anticipated tyranny, corruption, foreign invasion, and mob rule.

17
00:01:42.160 --> 00:01:48.410
They did not anticipate Florida. What Democracy Looks Like From Space.

18
00:01:49.480 --> 00:01:56.580
Princeton University analyzed one thousand seven hundred and seventy-nine policy decisions over twenty years.

19
00:01:57.020 --> 00:02:04.060
The correlation between what ordinary voters want and what actually becomes law: ZERO PERCENT.

20
00:02:04.920 --> 00:02:14.340
Not "low." Not "disappointing." Zero. Your democracy correlates with your preferences the way a coin flip correlates with

21
00:02:14.340 --> 00:02:18.220
your breakfast. This was published in a peer-reviewed journal.

22
00:02:18.860 --> 00:02:26.960
It did not make the news, because a study proving democracy doesn't work is below the boredom threshold of a country that

23
00:02:27.160 --> 00:02:34.120
believes it has one. When economic elites want something, it happens seventy-eight percent of the time.

24
00:02:34.880 --> 00:02:42.720
Whether zero percent or one hundred percent of voters support a bill, it has the same roughly thirty percent chance of passing.

25
00:02:43.480 --> 00:02:52.480
The line is flat. Voter opinion enters the machine, the machine ignores it, and a law comes out the other end based on who

26
00:02:52.480 --> 00:02:57.600
paid for the machine. You have the same political influence as your houseplant.

27
00:02:58.340 --> 00:03:04.220
Less, actually. Your houseplant produces oxygen, which at least one congressman needs.

28
00:03:05.180 --> 00:03:11.860
I found this confusing at first. Then I looked at your institutions, and they had already told me.

29
00:03:12.600 --> 00:03:16.480
You have a "Department of Defense" that mainly attacks people.

30
00:03:17.080 --> 00:03:21.780
A "Department of Health" that has not yet produced any observable health.

31
00:03:22.220 --> 00:03:26.940
And a "Department of Justice" that... well. You can see where this is going.

32
00:03:27.980 --> 00:03:34.260
The Mathematics of Why Nobody Fixes Anything. Concentrated Benefits vs.

33
00:03:34.340 --> 00:03:40.160
Diffuse Costs. On Wishonia, we discovered this problem six thousand years ago.

34
00:03:40.800 --> 00:03:49.440
It goes like this: Sugar subsidies cost Americans three point five billion dollars per year, which is ten dollars per person.

35
00:03:50.060 --> 00:03:56.340
Will you take a day off work, drive to your capitol, and stand in a hallway to save ten dollars?

36
00:03:56.760 --> 00:04:03.020
No. Will sugar companies spend millions to keep their three point five billion dollars?

37
00:04:03.540 --> 00:04:09.540
Obviously. This is the entire explanation for why your government does what it does.

38
00:04:09.600 --> 00:04:15.380
One sentence. Everything else is details. The details are where the crime hides.

39
00:04:16.120 --> 00:04:22.320
On Wishonia, we call this "beige crime": theft so boring that nobody investigates it.

40
00:04:22.580 --> 00:04:29.940
Your best criminals discovered long ago that the boredom threshold of the average citizen is approximately ten dollars.

41
00:04:30.360 --> 00:04:36.520
Steal more than that from one person and they'll complain. Steal ten dollars from three hundred and thirty million people

42
00:04:36.520 --> 00:04:44.880
and you'll receive an industry award. The pattern: Pharma companies: five hundred billion dollars in concentrated profits.

43
00:04:45.560 --> 00:04:49.340
Patients: fifteen hundred dollars each in diffuse suffering.

44
00:04:49.800 --> 00:04:58.130
Result: Pharma gets what pharma wants. Banks in two thousand and eight: seven hundred billion dollar bailout (concentrated).

45
00:04:58.620 --> 00:05:04.700
Taxpayers: twenty-one hundred dollars each (diffuse). Result: Banks get what banks want.

46
00:05:05.740 --> 00:05:10.680
The Death Spiral: How Two Choices Kill Three Hundred and Thirty Million Options.

47
00:05:11.620 --> 00:05:20.040
A human named James Surowiecki demonstrated that crowds outperform experts under four conditions: diversity of opinion,

48
00:05:20.340 --> 00:05:25.380
independence of judgment, decentralization, and a good aggregation mechanism.

49
00:05:26.140 --> 00:05:31.500
Your democracy has three of these. It systematically destroys the most important one.

50
00:05:32.500 --> 00:05:38.240
Independence means each person decides based on their own knowledge, not by watching everyone else.

51
00:05:38.980 --> 00:05:46.580
Your voting system annihilates this. You have three hundred and thirty million people, many of whom would make excellent

52
00:05:46.580 --> 00:05:55.030
leaders. But you don't choose among them. You choose between two candidates pre-selected by a process optimized for fundraising,

53
00:05:55.400 --> 00:05:59.340
name recognition, and not offending people who vote in primaries.

54
00:05:59.900 --> 00:06:04.020
Then, even between those two, you don't vote for the one you like.

55
00:06:04.480 --> 00:06:12.260
You vote against the one you fear. Voters who prefer a third candidate vote for a major-party candidate they dislike to

56
00:06:12.260 --> 00:06:20.240
avoid "wasting" their vote. This is called "strategic voting," and it means your ballot doesn't measure what you want.

57
00:06:20.660 --> 00:06:25.270
It measures what you think other people will do. That is not independence.

58
00:06:26.020 --> 00:06:31.520
That is a herd. On Wishonia, we observed this same phenomenon in army ants.

59
00:06:32.240 --> 00:06:38.500
Army ants are nearly blind. They navigate by following pheromone trails laid by the ants ahead of them.

60
00:06:39.120 --> 00:06:45.260
When a foraging column gets separated from the main trail, the leading ants have nothing to follow, so they wander.

61
00:06:45.740 --> 00:06:52.800
But they're still laying pheromone as they walk. If the front of the column curves enough to cross its own trail, the leaders

62
00:06:52.800 --> 00:07:01.940
pick up that signal and follow it. The circle closes. Every ant is now both following and reinforcing the path that traps

63
00:07:01.940 --> 00:07:08.460
it. They march in a loop, sometimes hundreds of thousands strong, until they die of exhaustion.

64
00:07:09.180 --> 00:07:17.300
No individual ant is stupid. Each is following a perfectly rational rule: follow the strongest signal.

65
00:07:17.920 --> 00:07:26.460
The system is stupid. The circle emerges not from bad ants but from a structure where no ant can navigate independently.

66
00:07:27.100 --> 00:07:34.560
Your two-party system is a death spiral with stickers. Each voter follows the signal of viability rather than their own

67
00:07:34.560 --> 00:07:41.300
judgment. Pollsters tell you who's "electable." Media covers the two "real" candidates.

68
00:07:41.320 --> 00:07:47.900
Donors fund the two "viable" options. You vote for one of them because everyone else is voting for one of them because everyone

69
00:07:47.900 --> 00:07:53.700
else is voting for one of them. The best candidate out of three hundred and thirty million people could be standing right

70
00:07:53.700 --> 00:08:00.020
there. But if they're not wearing the right color tie, they're "throwing their vote away," which is a phrase that only makes

71
00:08:00.020 --> 00:08:07.720
sense in a system designed to waste votes. The result: a nation of three hundred and thirty million, selecting from a talent

72
00:08:07.720 --> 00:08:14.860
pool of three hundred and thirty million, reliably produces a choice between two people that approximately nobody is enthusiastic

73
00:08:14.860 --> 00:08:19.200
about. Each ant follows the one ahead. The leader follows the last.

74
00:08:19.820 --> 00:08:26.080
They march in a circle until they die. Your two-party system works the same way, but the circle takes four years.

75
00:08:31.260 --> 00:08:31.260
(Clemzouzou sixty-nine, C.C. B.Y. S.A. four point zero). How Money Buys Your Government.

76
00:08:31.260 --> 00:08:33.720
Campaign contributions don't directly buy how politicians vote.

77
00:08:34.299 --> 00:08:39.320
Only one in four studies support that. The system is much more elegant than bribery.

78
00:08:39.840 --> 00:08:45.920
On most planets, this would just be called bribery. On Earth, it's called "campaign finance," because giving it a different

79
00:08:45.920 --> 00:08:52.640
name apparently makes it legal. What money actually buys is which politicians exist in the first place.

80
00:08:53.560 --> 00:08:58.500
Ninety-five percent of House races since two thousand and four were won by the highest spender.

81
00:08:59.020 --> 00:09:03.660
The average winning House campaign costs two point seven nine million dollars.

82
00:09:04.120 --> 00:09:09.320
The average winning Senate campaign costs twenty-six point five three million dollars.

83
00:09:09.780 --> 00:09:13.980
Why bribe a politician when you can manufacture one who already agrees with you?

84
00:09:14.800 --> 00:09:20.290
On Wishonia, we had a saying for this: "why train the dog when you can buy a dog that already sits?".

85
00:09:20.960 --> 00:09:27.320
For the ones already in office, contributions buy something more useful than votes: Access means your call gets returned

86
00:09:27.320 --> 00:09:34.320
while everyone else gets voicemail. Agenda control ensures your issue gets a hearing while everyone else's gets a filing

87
00:09:34.320 --> 00:09:40.720
cabinet. Legislative language provides the exact wording that creates the exact loopholes you need.

88
00:09:41.380 --> 00:09:48.560
Committee positions cost four hundred and fifty thousand dollars to party committees to buy a seat where bills live or die.

89
00:09:48.940 --> 00:09:53.500
You don't buy the vote. You buy the voter, the agenda, and the room where the vote happens.

90
00:09:54.080 --> 00:10:02.060
Then the vote takes care of itself. The Lobbying R.O.I.: Better Than Every Other Investment.

91
00:10:02.060 --> 00:10:07.580
The most profitable investment on your planet is not stocks, real estate, or cryptocurrency.

92
00:10:08.020 --> 00:10:13.320
It's congressmen: Disease advocacy groups spend one hundred million dollars on lobbying.

93
00:10:13.680 --> 00:10:18.380
In return, they receive one point eight billion dollars in increased funding.

94
00:10:18.780 --> 00:10:23.280
That is a return of eighteen to one (seventeen hundred percent net).

95
00:10:23.880 --> 00:10:29.660
Your stock market returns about ten percent per year. Your congressmen return seventeen hundred percent.

96
00:10:30.280 --> 00:10:35.040
This is not a metaphor. These are the actual numbers. The only reason more people don't invest in congressmen is that the

97
00:10:35.040 --> 00:10:40.870
minimum buy-in requires a trade association, which requires being rich, which is the entire point.

98
00:10:41.540 --> 00:10:50.260
Meanwhile, your disease funding is allocated by lobbying power, not by who's dying: Breast cancer has good lobbyists and

99
00:10:50.260 --> 00:10:56.280
massive funding. Heart disease kills more people, but has worse lobbyists and less funding.

100
00:10:56.880 --> 00:11:05.080
Rare pediatric diseases have no lobbyists and no funding. If your disease can't hire a lobbying firm, it isn't a real disease

101
00:11:05.080 --> 00:11:11.380
as far as your government is concerned. It's just dying, which doesn't have a PAC.

102
00:11:11.380 --> 00:11:15.220
Your Congressman: A Fundraiser Who Occasionally Legislates.

103
00:11:16.340 --> 00:11:19.560
I studied how your elected representatives spend their time.

104
00:11:20.200 --> 00:11:28.860
I assumed they spent it representing. I was incorrect. Average time congressmen spend on: They spend four hours per day

105
00:11:28.860 --> 00:11:33.320
fundraising. They spend zero hours per day reading bills they vote on.

106
00:11:34.080 --> 00:11:38.280
Actual governing is whatever is left after calling rich people for money.

107
00:11:39.040 --> 00:11:46.960
They don't read what they vote on. The PATRIOT Act: three hundred and forty-two pages, voted on forty-five minutes after

108
00:11:46.960 --> 00:11:53.520
release. That's seven point six pages per minute. Olympic speed-readers cannot do this.

109
00:11:54.140 --> 00:12:01.620
The Affordable Care Act: two thousand seven hundred pages, described by the Speaker of the House as something "we have to

110
00:12:01.620 --> 00:12:10.140
pass to see what's in it". On Wishonia, if you approved a two thousand seven hundred-page document without reading it, you

111
00:12:10.140 --> 00:12:16.520
would not be allowed to operate heavy machinery. On Earth, these people control nuclear weapons.

112
00:12:17.280 --> 00:12:22.800
Congressional Committees: The Price List. Committees control which bills live or die.

113
00:12:23.540 --> 00:12:30.020
Both parties assign fundraising quotas for each seat: raise this much money for the party, or lose your assignment.

114
00:12:30.620 --> 00:12:37.340
Don't hit your number? Your bills die, your amendments vanish, and your name goes on a literal "wall of shame".

115
00:12:38.080 --> 00:12:45.560
Here is the menu. It has prices, like a restaurant, except the food is legislation and the waiters have to buy their own

116
00:12:45.560 --> 00:12:54.340
sections. The House Speaker position controls the entire legislative agenda and costs thirty-one million dollars for the

117
00:12:54.340 --> 00:13:02.140
Democratic Congressional Campaign Committee, or D.C.C.C., and twenty million dollars for the National Republican Congressional

118
00:13:02.140 --> 00:13:11.260
Committee, or N.R.C.C. The Ways and Means Chair controls all tax laws and costs one point eight million dollars for Democrats

119
00:13:11.260 --> 00:13:19.640
and one point two million dollars for Republicans. The Appropriations Chair controls a one point seven trillion dollar budget

120
00:13:19.900 --> 00:13:25.260
and costs one point eight million dollars for Democrats and one point two million dollars for Republicans.

121
00:13:25.840 --> 00:13:25.920
The Energy and Commerce Chair controls healthcare, energy, and telecom, and costs one point eight million dollars for Democrats

122
00:13:25.920 --> 00:13:29.840
and one point two million dollars for Republicans. A Regular Committee seat costs between five hundred thousand and one

123
00:13:29.840 --> 00:13:34.960
million dollars for Democrats and eight hundred and seventy-five thousand dollars for Republicans.

124
00:13:37.480 --> 00:13:37.600
The entry fee for a Basic Member is at least one hundred and fifty thousand dollars for Democrats and varies for Republicans.

125
00:13:37.600 --> 00:13:39.160
This is why congressmen spend four hours a day fundraising.

126
00:13:39.640 --> 00:13:42.940
They're not campaigning. They're paying rent on their committee seats.

127
00:13:43.760 --> 00:13:49.260
The people writing your healthcare laws bought their positions from the industries those laws regulate.

128
00:13:49.880 --> 00:13:56.220
On your planet, this is called "the system working.". On Wishonia, this is called "the system.".

129
00:13:57.280 --> 00:14:01.340
Public Choice Theory (Or: Why It Doesn't Matter Who You Elect).

130
00:14:02.440 --> 00:14:11.020
In nineteen eighty-six, a human named James Buchanan won what is technically not a Nobel Prize (see central banks chapter)

131
00:14:11.260 --> 00:14:18.480
in Economics for formally proving something that anyone who has visited your Department of Motor Vehicles already knew:

132
00:14:19.080 --> 00:14:22.830
Humans do not become better humans when you give them a government ID badge.

133
00:14:23.460 --> 00:14:30.940
This is Public Choice Theory. Bureaucrats, politicians, and regulators are exactly as selfish as everyone else.

134
00:14:31.700 --> 00:14:36.080
The only difference is that when a corporation is greedy, you can stop buying its product.

135
00:14:36.800 --> 00:14:41.700
When a government agency is greedy, it can arrest you for not buying its product.

136
00:14:42.740 --> 00:14:51.660
What Each Institution Actually Optimizes For. The N.I.H. is supposed to provide cures, but it focuses on grants awarded,

137
00:14:52.080 --> 00:15:00.660
resulting in no cures. The F.D.A. is supposed to save lives, but it focuses on avoiding blame, resulting in deadly delays.

138
00:15:01.540 --> 00:15:09.000
Congress is supposed to look out for voter welfare, but it focuses on campaign donations, resulting in corporate welfare.

139
00:15:09.860 --> 00:15:18.300
Pharma is supposed to improve health outcomes, but it focuses on recurring revenue, resulting in chronic disease being preferred.

140
00:15:18.980 --> 00:15:26.260
Defense contractors are supposed to provide national security, but they focus on government contracts, resulting in endless

141
00:15:26.260 --> 00:15:32.260
wars. Nobody in this system is incentivized to cure disease or end war.

142
00:15:32.960 --> 00:15:40.520
You could elect five hundred and thirty-five clones of your most idealistic human and within two years they'd be raising

143
00:15:40.520 --> 00:15:47.980
money from pharmaceutical companies, because the system requires it and the system does not care who you put inside it.

144
00:15:48.520 --> 00:15:53.580
It digests idealists the way a stomach digests food. Different inputs.

145
00:15:54.200 --> 00:16:02.280
Same output. The Paradox. Libertarians say: Government fails because government is inherently bad.

146
00:16:02.300 --> 00:16:08.310
(Solution: less government.). Socialists say: Government fails because it needs more resources.

147
00:16:08.800 --> 00:16:15.820
(Solution: more government.). Public Choice Theory says: Government fails because the people running it are people.

148
00:16:16.460 --> 00:16:23.120
(Solution: design systems where selfish behavior accidentally produces good outcomes.).

149
00:16:23.180 --> 00:16:29.340
On Wishonia, we went with the third option four thousand, two hundred and ninety-seven years ago.

150
00:16:29.760 --> 00:16:35.060
It took us twelve minutes to agree. You've been arguing about the first two for several centuries.

151
00:16:35.960 --> 00:16:43.500
Regulatory Capture: How the Burglar Trained the Guard Dog. There are thirteen thousand, seven hundred registered lobbyists

152
00:16:43.500 --> 00:16:50.520
in Washington. Half of them used to work in the government they're now lobbying, which on your planet is called "a career

153
00:16:50.520 --> 00:16:56.420
path." Corporations spend four point four billion dollars a year on this lobbying.

154
00:16:56.940 --> 00:17:04.520
Divide that by five hundred and thirty-five members of Congress and you get over eight million dollars in professional persuasion

155
00:17:04.520 --> 00:17:09.119
surrounding each congressman, who earns less than a mid-level dentist.

156
00:17:09.740 --> 00:17:14.380
The mystery is not that they're influenced. The mystery is that any of them aren't.

157
00:17:15.180 --> 00:17:22.460
A court ruling called Citizens United made corporations "people" who can donate unlimited money through Super PACs.

158
00:17:23.200 --> 00:17:29.970
Corporations are now legally people, which is interesting because you can't imprison a corporation, give it chemotherapy,

159
00:17:30.660 --> 00:17:37.740
or make it feel embarrassment. It has all the rights of a person and none of the vulnerabilities, which is a good deal if

160
00:17:37.740 --> 00:17:42.600
you can get it. Pharmaceutical companies write their own regulations.

161
00:17:43.040 --> 00:17:48.500
The Medicare Modernization Act banned the government from negotiating drug prices.

162
00:17:49.240 --> 00:17:56.080
This is why insulin that costs six dollars to make costs you three hundred dollars.

163
00:17:56.600 --> 00:18:05.500
A four thousand nine hundred percent markup. I checked with your illegal drug dealers, and even they find this excessive.

164
00:18:06.460 --> 00:18:16.300
The Self-Sustaining War Machine. The military-industrial complex distributes operations across forty-two states so that

165
00:18:16.300 --> 00:18:22.800
cutting any program threatens jobs in enough congressional districts to make it politically impossible.

166
00:18:23.980 --> 00:18:30.550
Cutting a weapons program doesn't just lose a contract. It loses votes in forty-two states simultaneously.

167
00:18:31.560 --> 00:18:39.720
This is not an accident. Someone designed this. But the remarkable part is not that the war industry lobbies for budgets.

168
00:18:40.420 --> 00:18:46.320
It's that the war industry produces the threats that justify the budgets.

169
00:18:46.460 --> 00:18:53.920
Robert Pape at the University of Chicago found that ninety-five percent of suicide terrorist attacks from nineteen eighty

170
00:18:53.920 --> 00:19:00.060
to two thousand and three were driven by foreign military occupation, not ideology.

171
00:19:00.960 --> 00:19:10.420
Half the attackers were secular. Your eight trillion dollars War on Terror took terrorist attacks from roughly one thousand

172
00:19:10.420 --> 00:19:20.660
per year to nearly seventeen thousand by twenty fourteen. Let me say that slowly: Military bases abroad create resistance.

173
00:19:22.060 --> 00:19:28.080
Resistance becomes terrorism. Terrorism justifies military budgets.

174
00:19:28.200 --> 00:19:36.120
Military budgets fund more bases abroad. Most industries merely buy favorable rules.

175
00:19:36.700 --> 00:19:42.280
The defense industry manufactures the emergencies that make the rules seem necessary.

176
00:19:43.020 --> 00:19:48.680
The Pentagon has never passed an audit. It cannot tell you where the last trillion dollars went.

177
00:19:49.360 --> 00:19:57.640
It is, however, very confident about needing the next one. Pharma employs three lobbyists for every member of Congress.

178
00:19:58.160 --> 00:20:03.920
The F.D.A. gets seventy-five percent of its drug review budget from the companies it regulates.

179
00:20:04.620 --> 00:20:10.580
For a detailed examination of what this produces, see F.D.A.: Unsafe and Ineffective.

180
00:20:11.580 --> 00:20:18.540
The Ratchet. The ratchet only turns one way. After World War Two, the U.S.

181
00:20:18.580 --> 00:20:24.190
cut military spending by eighty-seven point six percent in two years.

182
00:20:24.320 --> 00:20:27.700
G.M. went from building bombers back to building Cadillacs.

183
00:20:28.340 --> 00:20:36.540
The economy boomed. It was the greatest period of broad-based prosperity in your country's history, and it happened immediately

184
00:20:36.540 --> 00:20:45.220
after you stopped spending money on killing people. The military-industrial complex made sure that never happened again.

185
00:20:46.000 --> 00:20:55.180
In inflation-adjusted dollars, peacetime spending is now thirty point six times the pre-World War Two baseline: twenty-nine

186
00:20:55.180 --> 00:21:03.640
billion dollars in nineteen thirty-nine versus eight hundred and eighty-six billion dollars today, in constant dollars,

187
00:21:04.080 --> 00:21:13.370
with no world war to justify it. The tumor grew its own blood supply and then reclassified the growth as vital organs.

188
00:21:13.960 --> 00:21:20.760
The Equilibrium. Cures are a one-time payment. Treatments are recurring revenue.

189
00:21:21.500 --> 00:21:30.280
Approving a bad drug ends careers. Delaying a good one ends lives, but not careers, and the people making the decisions

190
00:21:30.280 --> 00:21:36.060
have careers, not diseases. Everyone who decides things is winning.

191
00:21:36.560 --> 00:21:41.520
Everyone who's dying is not deciding things. The system works perfectly.

192
00:21:42.080 --> 00:21:49.680
It is just optimized for the wrong species. On Wishonia, we noticed the same pattern six thousand years ago.

193
00:21:50.540 --> 00:21:57.600
The solution was not to elect better people. (We tried. They got eaten by the system in about twelve minutes.) The solution

194
00:21:57.600 --> 00:22:02.880
was to build a different system. The Solution They Don't Want You to Have.

